From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:39:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(players): render server player controllers and costumes

A server can replace minecraft:player with property-driven geometry,
textures and animation controllers. Evaluate that graph against the
native actor snapshot, retain the player's supplied skin model, and render
selected costume surfaces with their material and UV animation bindings.
Keep mutable controller variables per player and release server textures
when the pack stack changes. Report unresolved active costume assets.

Saved CubeCraft packs declare hats, gloves, shoes and back accessories;
controller tests cover enum properties, visibility, UV frame selection and
conditional pre_animation assignments. Geometry tests preserve authored
bones under the standard humanoid alias and retain no-data fallback.

Compile initialize and pre-animation arrays as coherent bounded programs.
Preserve conditional scopes and early returns across JSON fragments, run
initialization once, and evaluate pre-animation once per frame. Cache the
compiled immutable script arrays without changing expression evaluation.
Semantic tests cover initialization scope and repeated frame updates.

Import the shared typed-value evaluator within this renderer feature. The
base actor parser now applies bounded native expression precedence directly
and no longer needs the generic Mocha operator normalization import.

Bind server player sound aliases to the effective server pack stack. Gather
bounded catalog and supported sample files in the existing bottom-to-top
order, then reuse the shared typed sound resolver and PCM playback path.
Server overrides can replace catalog entries without copying lower sample
files. This keeps sound resources with the immutable graph that selected
them and avoids an unrelated built-in catalog fallback. Controller entry
sounds, costume locator selection and missing native sample references
remain separate coverage gaps.

Broaden the server effect library to particle definitions and their referenced
PNG/TGA textures. Resolve identifiers and images through server pack order,
including upper texture overrides for lower definitions. Retain the existing
file and byte limits across all effect resources and omit unrelated images.
A targeted test verifies emission-count overrides and PNG-to-TGA replacement.

Keep missing particle texture placeholders from aborting the whole server
player graph. CubeCraft's saved 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) pack declares
an unused zombie-hands emitter with texture "_". Collect bounded relative
references and return no image for missing files; preserve traversal checks,
pack precedence and resource limits. This does not broaden runtime support
for particle events or materials.

Evidence: saved scene SHA-256
f75e056dbf5d80850cae93fe36891f49b12fca309a23b257b4efd378620638ef.
Nine resource-library tests pass with licensed fixtures, including missing
placeholders, supported emission, image lookup and traversal rejection.

Retain root sounds.json with server effect resources so particle level
sound events resolve through the same effective stack as their emitters.
Reuse the library's bounded generic configuration reader. Extend the
existing override integration test to select an upper configuration and
decode its sample from a lower pack, preserving each resource's order.

Keep loose WAV samples in the bounded server actor effect library as well
as the server playback archive. Actor aliases and particle sound events
can then resolve a lower-pack WAV after an upper catalog override, using
the shared native decoder and the existing pack precedence.

Retain language files alongside server particle sound catalogs, so captions
use the same pack precedence as their local source definitions. Consume
metadata selection separately from sample decoding in effect-library tests.

Select direct server effects through the same bounded core sound, caption, and particle APIs used by converted packs. Preserve every pack index and texture-only overlays without an add-on selector or ZIP roundtrip. Validate texture references when their effect is consumed so an unused malformed definition does not prevent unrelated effects from loading.

Resolve server player overrides from the accepted native actor archive on
both connection routes. Preserve the producer's server provenance so its
built-in player defaults do not become a server costume. Graph and texture
reloads now use the same frontend resource lifetime through ViaProxy.
Proxy actor properties and emote state remain separate transport work.

Apply the server controller's body visibility to animated persona surfaces
as well as the base skin. The target 1.26.51.1 reference uses a persona skin
with a separate animated face model. Its server costume replaces that body
without the original face. Java previously drew the face over the costume
because only the base skin's render type respected the controller decision.
Preserve the face and other persona surfaces when the original body remains
selected. First-person selection remains a separate unverified requirement.

Load the accepted built-in player graph when a server animation command
requires it, even for classic skins without a server costume. Keep server
provenance separate from graph availability, so this never turns built-in
player definitions into a costume override. Reevaluate graph demand when
commands arrive after an earlier vanilla renderer lookup.

Resolve first-person costume surfaces in the sampled actor scope after
animation and scale, without a second script pass. Use the shared selector
for geometry, textures, wildcard visibility, lighting and supported native
alpha-test materials. Each selected controller retains its own hierarchy;
original skin selections retain persona surfaces and replacement bodies
omit them. Clear hand models with pack changes and preserve full images
for shader UV transforms.

The matching player definition selects separate first-person controllers.
Native render pass 0x141c362e0 evaluates selected controllers independently.
Per-bone materials, other material families, effects, equipment and complete
visible timing remain separate work.

Reuse each controller declaration's compiled visibility pattern when
creating first-person surfaces. Humanoid models use the same scoped name
matching as their actor geometry, without compiling another regex during
each frame. Exact custom actor patterns and intentional controller hides
retain their existing behavior.

Unlit first-person surfaces submit full-bright packed light so the ordinary
Iris cutout program can bypass world light without invoking glowing eyes.

Provide the player head_x_rotation query to both server skin selection and
costume animation graphs. The native 1.26.51.1 client (build 51061372,
protocol 2193) truncates the numeric head index and returns player pitch
for head zero. Other indices and invalid arguments do not select a head.
Use the extracted player pitch, preserving world interpolation and the
native GUI render state.

CubeCraft's ui_render animation counter-rotates the waist with this query.
A missing query silently returned zero, leaving the portrait body tilted
and its head displaced when the preview camera pitched. Native GUI bone
captures confirm opposite waist and camera rotations of 23.588333 degrees.
The live native and add-on comparison now places the head at the same
vertical origin. Targeted tests cover argument handling and the authored
waist transform across positive, zero, and negative camera pitch. Other
head queries and costume posture comparisons remain separate coverage.

Reuse parsed immutable built-in model declarations when creating a server overlay. Parse only newly accepted layers and retain highest-pack identifier replacement. Each model lookup still returns an isolated copy; actor graph parsing and texture precedence remain unchanged.

Inherit the bundled Bedrock 1.26.51.1 player description, animations and
render controllers before accepted server assets. Animation-only and
render-controller-only packs override the native resources without needing
to repeat player.entity.json. Normalize only the actor's referenced models
and retain server provenance and skin animation aliases. Load the resolved
dependencies off the render thread and invalidate them with the resource
generation. Ordinary classic owner graphs now also receive armor setup.
